cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below, together with ANKYRA (listed under references)."
type: software
title: "Building_load_ANKYRA_lite (ankyra-lite): month-ahead hourly load forecasting with a small self-developed carrier network and the ANKYRA anchoring layer"
abstract: >-
  ankyra-lite is a self-contained month-ahead (744-hour) hourly load forecaster for buildings, industrial meters and
  households that needs no foundation model. A small self-developed carrier network (a copy-pointer structured load
  network with 61,613 parameters, trained per population) forecasts the month after a forecast origin from the unit's
  own history and the supplied calendar, subject to the documented calendar-availability exceptions. Version 0.2.0 averages six seeds of the carrier, each trained with every
  training window's loss weighted by a frozen inverse-noise rule (recipe Rw), at every issue time. Its forecasts at
  the origin and at up to six earlier pseudo-origins are passed to an unchanged copy of the ANKYRA 2.2.1 anchoring
  layer, which estimates per-unit combination weights from the unit's own completed pseudo-forecasts; a
  seasonal-naive state rule handles off-state windows, and a new origin-continuity layer (four frozen constants)
  applies a decaying correction across the anchored horizon, strongest in the first hours and days, toward the last observed hours. The month's energy,
  level and monthly peak readout are read before the continuation layer (readout rule A). The 0.1.x configuration
  (recipe R, its three shipped seeds, the continuation layer off, with the same inputs) stays available for reproducing the forecasts of 0.1.1.
  The repository contains the
  package, its training recipes, equivalence tests against the research code and the original ANKYRA
  package, the evaluation code, a re-evaluation against 21 baselines (22 forecasters) on nine populations (hourly,
  energy, peak, conventional metrics, forecast-day, error-block, scaled-error and rank-test tables; test windows read
  before, so a description, not a first test), and external tests re-run once on four previously read test sets.
